#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# Getting started

#% text
# The notebook, how an input is evaluated, exact and numeric results, variables and functions, and typed mathematics.

#% section
# The notebook and evaluation

#% text
# A notebook is one `.mcn` file of cells. Type an input and press Shift+Enter: the cell is evaluated and its value appears below it. Evaluation menu: evaluate all, abort. Several inputs in one cell run in order; the value of the last one is shown, and `print` lines appear before it.

#% cell
1/3 + 1/6
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
2^100
#% output
#= 1267650600228229401496703205376

#% cell
print("hello"); 6*7
#% output
#| hello
#= 42

#% text
# Results stay exact until you ask for numbers: `sqrt(8)` is `2*sqrt(2)`, and `pi` stays `pi`. `n` turns a value into a number (machine precision by default; the guide on precision and numerics gives the rest).

#% cell
sqrt(8) + sqrt(2)
#% output
#= 3*2^(1/2)

#% cell
n(pi)
#% output
#= 3.141592653589793

#% section
# Variables, functions and lists

#% text
# `=` assigns. A function is defined with `fn`; the right side may use any expression. Lists are written in square brackets, and arithmetic works on them element by element.

#% cell
a = 5; a^2 + 1
#% output
#= 26

#% cell
fn f(x) = x^2 + 1

#% cell
f(3)
#% output
#= 10

#% cell
f(y + 1)
#% output
#= 1 + (1 + y)^2

#% cell
map(f, [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [2, 5, 10]

#% cell
[1, 2, 3] + [10, 20, 30]
#% output
#= [11, 22, 33]

#% text
# `table` builds a list from a rule: `table(e, k, n)` runs `k` from 1 to `n`, `table(e, k, list)` over the elements of a list, and `table(e, [k, a, b, step])` from `a` to `b`. A vector combined with every row of a matrix is marked `threaded`; the guide on lists and data has more.

#% cell
table(k^2, k, 5)
#% output
#= [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]

#% cell
table(k^2, [k, 0, 10, 5])
#% output
#= [0, 25, 100]

#% cell
[[1, 2], [3, 4]] + threaded([10, 20])
#% output
#= [[11, 22], [13, 24]]

#% text
# Symbols that have no value stay symbols, so you can work with formulas right away.

#% cell
expand((x + 1)^3)
#% output
#= 1 + 3*x + 3*x^2 + x^3

#% cell
d(sin(x)*x, x)
#% output
#= x*cos(x) + sin(x)

#% section
# Typed mathematics

#% text
# Inputs may be written as mathematics. Ctrl+/ makes a fraction, Ctrl+6 a power, Ctrl+2 a square root and Ctrl+5 an n-th root, edited in place; Ctrl+Shift+V pastes text as written mathematics. Such an input is the same expression as the linear text, so `x^2/(1 + x)` typed with boxes or as text is the same input. Press `F1` on a name for its documentation page, and Ctrl+click on a name opens its page too.

#% section
# Common mistakes

#% item
# `x(x - k)` is a call of `x` on the argument `x - k`, not a product. Products need `*`: write `x*(x - k)`. The editor underlines a call of a variable and offers the fix.

#% cell
x(x - k)
#% output
#= x(x - k)

#% cell
x*(x - k)
#% output
#= x*(x - k)

#% item
# A space between two operands is a product, as on paper: `2x`, `2 x` and `x y` mean `2*x`, `2*x` and `x*y`, and the editor shows a faint × in the space. A call has no space before its bracket: `f(x)` calls `f`, `f (x)` is `f*x`.

#% cell
2x + 3x
#% output
#= 5*x

#% item
# The imaginary unit is `im`, not `i`: `(1 + 2im)^2`.

#% cell
(1 + 2im)^2
#% output
#= -3 + 4im

#% item
# Equations use `==`; a single `=` assigns. `solve(x^2 == 4, x)` and not `solve(x^2 = 4, x)`.

#% cell
solve(x^2 == 4, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -2], [x -> 2]]

#% section
# Related functions

#% text
# `n`, `expand`, `d`, `solve`, `map`, `print`, `table`, `threaded`
